from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP

mcp = FastMCP(
    name="nina-planner",
    instructions="""Provides tools for controlling NINA (Nighttime Imaging 'N' Astronomy) software run observatories. Lets you inspect equipment, get observatory setup, write observation plans, and run NINA sequences generated from the plans. Focuses on orchestrating observation plans at a high level through NINA sequences rather than managing the individual commands that make up those sequences. Safety semantics: the safety monitor's is_safe field reflects whether the observatory enclosure (roof/dome) is open and it is safe to unpark and expose. is_safe=true means the enclosure is open and the scope may be unparked and sky-dependent acquisition may proceed; is_safe=false means the enclosure is closed, so the scope must remain parked and sky-dependent acquisition (lights, dawn/dusk sky flats) is gated until it becomes safe. Darks and biases are NOT sky-dependent and are exempt from that gate: they may be loaded and started with the enclosure closed, because the dark/bias step parks the mount first and never unparks, cuts the light at the camera (Dark filter slot, cover, or shutter), and needs no open sky. This is distinct from weather conditions, which are reported separately by the weather device. Park is the only closing maneuver: sequences emit Park Scope whenever the mount reports can_park=true, and a mount that cannot park is rejected at load time. Find home is never a park fallback: it is emitted once per lights step, ahead of set_tracking, when the mount reports can_find_home=true. Homing is a calibration — the mount drives its axes to a known physical reference point (the home position) and re-syncs its sensors, about 30 seconds — and it is what restores a trustworthy position readout after the mount loses sync (a power outage, a bump, a driver restart). Park is the closing maneuver; homing is not. Lifecycle: start_nina() launches NINA into the active interactive desktop session; stop_nina() terminates NINA immediately. If NINA is already running, start_nina will error and require calling stop_nina first. get_nina_status() reports authoritative REST-API liveness (running), whether this host can control NINA's lifecycle (lifecycle_control_available), and a one-line summary (cheaper than get_site_equipment_status and never raises on a down NINA). When lifecycle_control_available is false — e.g. the MCP server is remote from NINA — start_nina/stop_nina cannot operate on NINA: a down NINA must be restarted on its own host, and an already-running NINA cannot be stopped from here. Progress tracking: maintain an observatory progress file (PROGRESS.md in the project directory). On session start, read it to restore context before acting. After significant actions — status checks, plan writes, sequence loads/starts/stops, errors, and interventions — append a short timestamped entry (ISO-8601 timestamp) recording what was done, the observed equipment and safety state, and any decisions. Both the active session and automated worker sessions append to this file so history is shared across sessions.""",
)
